.TH "GIT-BUG" "1" "Apr 2019" "Generated from git-bug's source code" ""

.SH NAME
git-bug-bug-new - Create a new bug


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgit-bug bug new [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a new bug


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-t\fP, \fB--title\fP=""
	Provide a title to describe the issue

.PP
\fB-m\fP, \fB--message\fP=""
	Provide a message to describe the issue

.PP
\fB-F\fP, \fB--file\fP=""
	Take the message from the given file. Use - to read the message from the standard input

.PP
\fB--non-interactive\fP[=false]
	Do not ask for user input

.PP
\fB--pr\fP[=false]
	Create a pull-request instead of a plain issue. Requires --base and --head. If you later \fBgit bug push\fR to a GitHub bridge, the --head branch must already exist on the remote; git-bug does not push branches.

.PP
\fB--base\fP=""
	For --pr: the base branch the PR targets (e.g. refs/heads/main).

.PP
\fB--head\fP=""
	For --pr: the head branch the PR proposes to merge.

.PP
\fB--head-commit\fP=""
	For --pr: the head commit hash. Defaults to the current HEAD of --head if resolvable.

.PP
\fB--draft\fP[=false]
	For --pr: mark the PR as draft (not ready for review).

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for new


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgit-bug-bug(1)\fP
